  "operative_text": "SECTION 1 (new HRS 134-B): It shall be a violation of this part for a firearm industry member to fail to comply with any requirement of this part.\nSECTION 1 (new HRS 134-B): A firearm industry member shall: (1) Establish, implement, and enforce reasonable controls; (2) Take reasonable precautions to ensure that the firearm industry member does not sell, distribute, or provide to a downstream distributor a firearm-related product that is abnormally dangerous and likely to create an unreasonable risk of harm to public health and safety in the State; and (3) Not engage in any conduct related to the sale or marketing of firearm-related products that is in violation of this chapter.\nSECTION 1 (new HRS 134-C): An act or omission by a firearm industry member in violation of this part shall constitute an actionable cause of action.\nSECTION 1 (new HRS 134-C): A person who has suffered harm in the State because of a firearm industry member's violation of this part may bring an action in a court of competent jurisdiction.\nSECTION 1 (new HRS 134-C): In addition to any lawsuit filed against a firearm owner pursuant to section 663-9.5, the attorney general or any county attorney or public prosecutor may bring a civil action in a court of competent jurisdiction in the name of the people of the State to enforce this part and remedy harm caused by a violation of this part."
}